    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.indentscope\n\n### Description\n\nHello! I've been loving mini.indent lately, especially its `ii` indent textobject. Recently I've started to notice that it would be nice in a lot of cases to be able to have the textobject select by both indent level and paragraph.\n\nFor example, let's say your cursor is at the start of \"secretfun\" on line 3, and you want to change the block of code on lines 2-6:\n\n```lua\n1  function TestFun(arg)\n2    mysteryfun(arg)\n3    secretfun(arg)\n4    if arg.cool_attribute == nil then\n5      return \"no\"\n6    end\n7  \n8    unknownfun(arg)\n9    coolfun(arg)\n10 \n11   return tostring(arg)\n12 end\n```\n\nIn this case, using `cip` to change the paragraph is almost right, but it also selects the function declaration on line 1. Using mini.indentscope's `cii` is also close, but it would also select the rest of the function body.\n\nWhat's needed here is really the ~~union~~ intersection ([in the mathematical sense](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intersection_(set_theory))) of the `cip` and `cii` text objects.\n\nWhat I am requesting here is that mini.indentscope provide such a textobject, since it would be useful in many of the cases that its `ii` textobject is useful in. I could even see it being a good replacement for the `p` textobject, since indent is often a good indicator of paragraph boundaries too.\n\nMaybe this is a case where a separate plugin for making textobjects based on set operations of other textobjects would be cool. It'd probably be more complicated than just making this though.\n\nThoughts?",
